const historyRows = [
["Historical Inquiry","Evaluating primary sources","A historian finds a dated letter written by a participant during a protest and a later textbook summary. Which use of the letter is most defensible?",["Treat it as completely unbiased because it is old.","Use it as a primary source, considering the writer’s position, purpose and context, and compare it with other evidence.","Ignore all other sources because one letter proves every detail.","Assume the letter describes events the writer never witnessed."],1,"A primary source offers direct evidence but still needs contextual evaluation and corroboration."],
["Historical Inquiry","Oral history","An elder describes how a community responded to a drought decades ago. What should a researcher do to strengthen the account?",["Record the account respectfully, ask contextual questions and compare it with other oral, written or material evidence.","Change details to fit a preferred theory.","Assume memory can never be mistaken.","Publish private details without consent."],0,"Oral history preserves lived experience but benefits from careful questioning and corroboration."],
["Historical Inquiry","Chronology","A learner must explain how a policy led to later protests. Why is placing events in chronological order useful?",["It helps establish sequence and investigate possible links, though sequence alone does not prove causation.","It proves that the earliest event caused every later event.","It removes the need for evidence.","It makes dates irrelevant."],0,"Chronology clarifies sequence, but causal claims require additional evidence."],
["Historical Inquiry","Cause and consequence","A new tax is followed by protests, but records also show food prices rising and local leaders disputing the policy. Which explanation is strongest?",["The tax alone must explain every protest.","Several interacting causes may have contributed, so evidence should be weighed for each factor.","The protests had no causes.","Food prices cannot affect public behaviour."],1,"Historical events often have multiple causes that should be assessed rather than reduced to one factor."],
["Historical Inquiry","Bias and perspective","Two newspapers describe the same political meeting differently. What is the best way to assess their accounts?",["Choose the paper whose headline is more dramatic.","Compare evidence, ownership, audience, language and corroborating accounts.","Assume both are false because they disagree.","Use only the photograph caption."],1,"Perspective and editorial choices affect reporting; corroboration helps assess reliability."],
["Historical Inquiry","Archaeological evidence","Researchers find pottery, tools and house foundations at an old settlement. What can these remains help establish?",["Aspects of past technology, trade and daily life, interpreted with context and other evidence.","Every resident’s exact thoughts.","A complete record of every conversation.","The future population of the settlement with certainty."],0,"Material remains can reveal aspects of past life, but interpretations depend on context and evidence."],
["Historical Inquiry","Interpreting statistics","A chart shows school enrolment rising over ten years. What additional evidence would help explain why it rose?",["Only the chart’s background colour.","Policy changes, population data, school access and other relevant records from the same period.","The name of the chart designer only.","An unrelated weather forecast."],1,"Trends describe change; explanatory evidence is needed to identify contributing factors."],
["History and Society","Continuity and change","A community adopts mobile banking but continues to use a traditional savings group. What does this example illustrate?",["Modern technology always eliminates older practices.","Continuity and change can occur together as communities adapt practices.","Culture never changes.","Technology has no effect on society."],1,"Societies can adopt new methods while retaining familiar institutions or values."],
["History and Society","Trade and interaction","Historical records show a coastal settlement exchanging goods with inland communities and visitors from overseas. What is a likely broader consequence of such trade?",["Exchange of goods, ideas, languages and cultural practices.","Complete isolation from other communities.","The end of all local production.","No change in settlement life under any circumstances."],0,"Trade can facilitate material and cultural exchange, although effects vary by context."],
["History and Society","Colonial rule and resistance","A community resists a colonial policy that takes land and restricts movement. Which explanation best connects the grievances to resistance?",["The policy threatened livelihoods, autonomy and access to resources.","Resistance can only be caused by weather.","Land has no social or economic value.","Every community responded identically."],0,"Loss of land, autonomy and livelihoods can be important sources of resistance."],
["History and Society","Independence movements","A liberation movement gains support from workers, farmers, students and religious groups with different priorities. What may help explain the coalition?",["Groups may unite around a shared goal while retaining different interests.","All groups must have identical beliefs.","A coalition cannot include different social groups.","The movement has no common objective."],0,"Shared goals can create coalitions even when members’ motivations differ."],
["History and Society","Constitutional development","Why is a constitution important in a democratic state?",["It sets out the structure and powers of government and protects rights under the law.","It allows leaders to act without limits.","It replaces all public institutions with one person.","It removes citizens’ responsibilities."],0,"A constitution establishes public authority, limits power and protects rights and procedures."],
["Citizenship","Separation of powers","A minister is accused of misusing public funds. Why is independent investigation and judicial review important?",["It helps hold public officials accountable under established law and procedures.","It proves the minister is guilty before evidence is heard.","It allows officials to ignore courts.","It removes the need for financial records."],0,"Independent oversight and due process help ensure accountability and fairness."],
["Citizenship","Devolution and local services","Residents want a broken county road repaired. Which action is most constructive?",["Identify the responsible county office, submit evidence through an official channel and follow up transparently.","Destroy the road signs to attract attention.","Share an unverified accusation as fact.","Assume national and county responsibilities are identical."],0,"Knowing the responsible level of government and using evidence-based channels improves civic engagement."],
["Citizenship","Rights and responsibilities","A learner claims freedom of expression means they may publish another learner’s private medical information. What is the best response?",["All speech overrides every other right.","Expression must be exercised with respect for privacy, dignity and applicable law.","Privacy applies only to adults.","Digital posts have no consequences."],1,"Rights coexist; exercising one right does not automatically justify violating another person’s rights."],
["Citizenship","Fair elections","A candidate offers gifts in exchange for votes. Which principle is most directly undermined?",["Free and fair choice by voters.","The right to education only.","The rule that all candidates must have the same name.","The need for public information."],0,"Vote-buying can compromise free choice and electoral integrity."],
["Citizenship","Public participation","A county plans a market but has not consulted traders who will be relocated. What should happen before the final decision?",["Provide meaningful public participation, share relevant information and consider the concerns raised.","Proceed secretly because consultation delays projects.","Consult only the contractor.","Collect opinions after all options are closed."],0,"Meaningful participation allows affected people to inform decisions before they are finalised."],
["Citizenship","Accountability","A public project is reported complete, but residents find that key facilities are missing. What evidence is useful in raising the issue?",["Dated observations, project specifications, expenditure information where available and a formal complaint to the responsible body.","An anonymous insult without details.","A fabricated photograph.","A rumour repeated many times."],0,"Specific verifiable evidence helps oversight bodies investigate possible gaps."],
["Citizenship","Public resources","A school committee discovers that supplies purchased with public funds are missing from the inventory. What is the appropriate response?",["Document the discrepancy, secure records and follow the official reporting and audit process.","Destroy the inventory book.","Accuse a person publicly without checking facts.","Ignore the issue because the funds are public."],0,"Documentation and due process protect public resources and support a fair investigation."],
["Citizenship","Conflict resolution","Two communities disagree over access to a shared water point during drought. Which process is most likely to produce a durable agreement?",["Facilitate inclusive dialogue, share evidence about needs and agree on transparent schedules and dispute procedures.","Let the stronger group take all the water.","Spread rumours about the other community.","Avoid recording the agreement."],0,"Inclusive negotiation, evidence and clear rules can reduce conflict over scarce resources."],
["Citizenship","National unity and diversity","A school celebrates languages and traditions from different Kenyan communities. What is the most important civic benefit?",["It can promote mutual respect and a shared sense of belonging without requiring everyone to be identical.","It proves that all communities have the same history.","It should exclude minority traditions.","It removes the need to learn a common language."],0,"Respectful recognition of diversity can strengthen inclusion and shared citizenship."],
["Citizenship","Media literacy","A viral post claims that a new law has been passed but links to no official source. What should a responsible citizen do before sharing it?",["Check the official legal or government source and credible independent reporting.","Share it immediately because it is popular.","Edit the claim to sound more certain.","Assume all viral posts are official."],0,"Verification reduces the spread of misinformation about public affairs."],
["Citizenship","Corruption and integrity","A procurement officer is offered a private payment to favour one bidder. What is the ethical response?",["Accept it because the bidder may provide good goods.","Decline, disclose the approach through proper channels and apply fair procurement rules.","Hide the payment in the accounts.","Award the contract without comparing bids."],1,"Rejecting and reporting bribery protects fairness, public resources and trust."],
["Citizenship","Gender equality","A school club repeatedly gives leadership roles only to boys despite qualified girls applying. What principle is at stake?",["Equal opportunity and non-discrimination.","The principle that leadership must be inherited.","The right to exclude all applicants.","The idea that skills cannot be assessed."],0,"Fair selection should be based on relevant criteria rather than gender stereotypes."],
["Citizenship","Youth participation","Young people want safer pedestrian crossings near a school. Which civic action is most likely to be effective?",["Collect observations, document risk points, consult affected people and present a clear proposal to the responsible authority.","Paint road markings without permission on a busy highway.","Spread a false claim about accidents.","Wait for the problem to solve itself."],0,"Evidence-based advocacy gives decision-makers clear information and a practical request."],
["Citizenship","Rule of law","A popular leader asks supporters to ignore a court order they dislike. What principle should guide citizens?",["The law should be applied through lawful procedures, and court decisions challenged through appropriate legal channels.","Popularity places a leader above the law.","Only unpopular people must obey the law.","Violence is the only appeal process."],0,"Rule of law requires lawful processes and accountability, including lawful routes of appeal."],
["Citizenship","Access to information","A community wants to understand the budget allocated to a local water project. What is a suitable step?",["Use lawful public-information and participation channels to request the relevant budget and project records.","Hack an official’s private account.","Invent figures and present them as official.","Assume budgets must never be shared."],0,"Public records and lawful information channels support transparency and accountability."],
["History and Heritage","Cultural heritage","An old community building is threatened by redevelopment. What should planners do before deciding its future?",["Document its historical significance, consult the community and assess feasible preservation or adaptive reuse options.","Demolish it before anyone records it.","Assume old buildings have no value.","Move artefacts without inventory or permission."],0,"Documentation and consultation help balance heritage protection with development needs."],
["History and Heritage","Oral traditions","A proverb is used by different generations to advise cooperation. How can a researcher interpret its historical role responsibly?",["Examine its wording, context, variations and how people use it rather than assuming one fixed meaning.","Assume it has never changed.","Treat every proverb as a dated legal record.","Ignore the people who use it."],0,"Oral traditions can carry values and history, but meanings depend on context and use."],
["History and Heritage","Monuments and memory","A monument honours one group but omits another group’s contribution to a major event. What is a constructive response?",["Research the available evidence and facilitate an inclusive discussion about how public memory is represented.","Destroy the monument immediately without consultation.","Assume public monuments are always complete accounts.","Ban historical debate."],0,"Public memory can be examined critically using evidence and inclusive participation."],
["History and Heritage","Museums and artefacts","A museum receives an artefact with no reliable information about where it came from. What should curators do?",["Record its provenance uncertainty and investigate ownership and context before making strong claims.","Invent a dramatic origin story.","Sell it without checking legal ownership.","Label it with a random date."],0,"Provenance and context are essential for responsible interpretation and lawful stewardship."],
["History and Heritage","Historical interpretation","Two historians agree on the dates of an event but disagree about its significance. How can a learner assess their interpretations?",["Compare the evidence they use, their reasoning, context and alternative explanations.","Choose the historian with the longer book title.","Assume disagreement means both are lying.","Ignore the sources and choose a favourite."],0,"Interpretations can differ; evidence and reasoning provide a basis for evaluation."],
["Governance","Public policy","A policy improves access to schools but increases travel time for some rural learners. What is the best evaluation approach?",["Consider benefits, costs, who gains or loses, and possible mitigation using evidence.","Judge it only by the number of pages in the policy.","Assume one benefit proves there are no drawbacks.","Ignore rural learners because they are fewer."],0,"Policy evaluation should examine outcomes, distributional effects and practical alternatives."],
["Governance","Oversight institutions","Why should public accounts be reviewed by an independent audit process?",["To check whether funds were used and reported properly and identify weaknesses or irregularities.","To guarantee every project is perfect.","To replace all public services.","To prevent citizens from asking questions."],0,"Independent audits strengthen accountability by examining financial records and controls."],
["Governance","Citizen petitions","A petition asks a county assembly to improve drainage in a flood-prone area. Which information makes it stronger?",["A clear request, locations affected, evidence of flooding, signatures or support and a feasible recommendation.","Only insults directed at leaders.","An unrelated list of sports results.","A demand with no description of the problem."],0,"Specific evidence and a clear, feasible request help decision-makers assess a petition."],
["Governance","Public consultation","Officials hold a meeting but provide technical plans only one hour before it starts. What weakness does this create?",["Participants may lack time and accessible information to contribute meaningfully.","It guarantees that every resident understands the plan.","It removes all power from officials.","It proves that the plan is illegal in every case."],0,"Meaningful participation requires timely, understandable information and a real opportunity to respond."],
["Governance","Checks and balances","A body investigates complaints about decisions made by another public office. What is the main democratic value of this arrangement?",["It can provide oversight and a route to accountability beyond the office being questioned.","It guarantees that every complaint is correct.","It removes the need for evidence.","It makes public offices immune to review."],0,"Checks and balances can limit abuse of power and enable independent review."],
["Governance","Rule-making and fairness","A school changes a major rule but informs only one class after the change takes effect. What process would be fairer?",["Communicate the rule and rationale clearly to all affected learners before implementation where feasible, and allow questions.","Keep the rule secret to avoid discussion.","Apply it only to learners who did not hear about it.","Change it differently for every learner without explanation."],0,"Fair rule-making requires clarity, consistent communication and reasonable opportunities to understand requirements."],
["Citizenship","Responsible protest","Residents plan a peaceful march about unsafe roads. Which preparation best supports lawful and safe participation?",["Understand applicable requirements, plan safety and accessibility, communicate the purpose and avoid violence.","Block emergency access without a plan.","Spread false claims to attract attention.","Encourage participants to damage property."],0,"Peaceful civic action is safer and more effective when planned lawfully and responsibly."],
["Citizenship","Rights during public action","During a public meeting, a speaker is criticised for an unpopular opinion. Which response best supports democratic discussion?",["Respond to the argument with evidence and respect lawful rights rather than threats or intimidation.","Prevent all opposing views from being heard.","Publish the speaker’s private information.","Assume disagreement proves wrongdoing."],0,"Democratic dialogue protects lawful expression while allowing reasoned disagreement."],
["History and Society","Regional integration","Neighbouring countries cooperate on transport, trade and shared environmental problems. What is one potential benefit of regional integration?",["Coordinated policies and cross-border trade may improve opportunities and shared problem-solving.","It guarantees that countries have no disagreements.","It eliminates the need for national laws.","It prevents all movement of goods."],0,"Regional cooperation can create shared benefits, though outcomes depend on implementation."],
["History and Society","Historical causation","A new railway is followed by growth of a trading town. Which additional evidence would help assess whether the railway contributed to growth?",["Population, trade and employment records before and after construction, compared with other influences and nearby towns.","A photograph of a train alone.","A modern map with no dates.","The railway’s paint colour."],0,"Before-and-after and comparative evidence helps assess a possible causal relationship."],
["Citizenship","Community service","A youth group wants to improve access to a public library. Which project plan is most likely to have lasting value?",["Assess user needs, consult library staff, set measurable goals, assign roles, budget and monitor use.","Buy equipment without asking what is needed.","Run one event and never evaluate it.","Exclude people with disabilities from consultation."],0,"Needs assessment, stakeholder involvement and monitoring improve relevance and sustainability."],
["Citizenship","Public trust","A public official publishes clear project costs, progress updates and reasons for delays. What governance principle is being demonstrated?",["Transparency and accountability.","Secrecy as the default.","Discrimination.","Censorship."],0,"Transparent reporting allows the public to understand decisions and scrutinise progress."],
["Historical Inquiry","Comparing sources","A photograph shows a crowded rally, while official attendance figures are much lower. What is the best next step?",["Check the photograph’s date, location and framing, examine how the figures were counted and seek additional sources.","Assume the photograph proves the official figures are false.","Assume official figures are always correct.","Ignore the discrepancy."],0,"Different evidence may capture different things; methods and context should be investigated."],
["Citizenship","Ethical decision-making","A learner finds a wallet containing money and an identification card. Which action best reflects responsible citizenship?",["Keep the money because nobody saw it.","Use the identification to return it through a safe, appropriate channel and avoid exposing private details publicly.","Post the owner’s address online.","Discard the identification card."],1,"Returning property respectfully protects honesty and the owner’s privacy."]
["Governance","Budget priorities","A county receives a limited budget and must choose between repairing a damaged water system and building a decorative gate. Which decision rule is most defensible?",["Choose the project that benefits the official’s relatives.","Compare urgency, public benefit, legal duties, cost and evidence of need, and explain the decision transparently.","Choose the most visible project regardless of need.","Spend the whole budget before assessing costs."],1,"Public budgeting should weigh needs, benefits, obligations and evidence, with transparent reasons."],
["Citizenship","Voter participation","A first-time voter is unsure where and how to confirm registration details. What is the most reliable action?",["Rely on an anonymous social-media post.","Check official electoral commission information and use the authorised registration or verification process.","Share an identification number publicly.","Assume registration is automatic in every case."],1,"Official election information reduces the risk of misinformation and protects personal data."],
["Citizenship","Reconciliation and restorative practice","After a school-community dispute, both sides agree to meet, acknowledge harm and plan corrective actions. What approach does this most closely reflect?",["Restorative dialogue and reconciliation.","Retaliation.","Historical denial.","Avoidance of responsibility."],0,"Restorative processes focus on acknowledging harm, repairing relationships and preventing recurrence."],
["History and Heritage","National symbols and identity","A learner asks why national symbols are used during public ceremonies. Which explanation is most balanced?",["They can represent shared history and civic identity, while people may interpret them through diverse experiences.","They prove that all citizens have identical opinions.","They replace the need for laws and institutions.","They have no historical or civic meaning."],0,"National symbols can support shared identity while still being open to historical interpretation and inclusive discussion."]
];
