const creRows = [
["Bible and Interpretation","Contextual interpretation","A learner reads a biblical passage describing a community facing hunger. Which approach best supports responsible interpretation?",["Read one sentence in isolation and ignore the setting.","Consider the passage’s context, audience, central message and how it relates to the wider text.","Assume every detail is a modern legal instruction.","Choose the interpretation that supports a personal preference without evidence."],1,"Context, audience and the wider text help prevent misleading interpretations."],
["Bible and Interpretation","Parables and moral reasoning","A story describes a traveller helping an injured stranger from a group usually treated as an outsider. What ethical lesson is most directly illustrated?",["Help only people from one’s own group.","Show compassion across social boundaries and respond to need.","Avoid every person in difficulty.","Judge a person’s worth by wealth."],1,"The story challenges exclusion and emphasises practical compassion toward a neighbour in need."],
["Bible and Interpretation","Prophetic messages and justice","A biblical prophet criticises leaders who exploit poor people while performing public religious rituals. What issue is being highlighted?",["Religious practice should be separated from every ethical concern.","Worship is inconsistent when it ignores justice and care for vulnerable people.","Only public ceremonies matter.","Poverty proves that a person is dishonest."],1,"The prophetic critique connects genuine faith with justice and ethical conduct."],
["Bible and Interpretation","Using evidence from scripture","A learner claims that forgiveness means allowing repeated harm without any boundaries. Which response is most balanced?",["Forgiveness requires hiding every harmful act.","Forgiveness can be pursued alongside truth, accountability, safety and changed behaviour.","Forgiveness means revenge is always required.","Forgiveness removes the need for responsibility."],1,"Forgiveness and reconciliation do not require ignoring harm or abandoning safety and accountability."],
["Bible and Interpretation","Comparing passages","Two biblical passages emphasise care for neighbours in different situations. How should a learner compare them?",["Identify their contexts and common ethical themes, then explain differences using textual evidence.","Assume the passages must have identical wording.","Ignore the situations described.","Choose one passage and claim the other is false."],0,"Comparison should consider context, shared themes and textual support."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Creation and stewardship","A school community is planning to clear a small wetland for a playground. Which response best reflects responsible stewardship of creation?",["Clear it immediately because land has no ecological value.","Assess ecological impacts, consider alternatives and protect the environment while meeting community needs.","Dump waste into the wetland first.","Assume stewardship means humans may use resources without limits."],1,"Stewardship involves responsible care and wise use of the natural world."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Human dignity","A new learner is mocked because of a disability. What response best reflects Christian teaching about human dignity?",["Join the mockery to fit in.","Challenge the exclusion respectfully, support the learner and help create an inclusive environment.","Ignore the situation because the learner is new.","Tell the learner to leave the school."],1,"Respect for each person’s dignity calls for inclusion and protection from humiliation."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Honesty and integrity","A learner finds a phone in the classroom and knows the owner’s identity. Which action best demonstrates integrity?",["Keep it because no one witnessed the discovery.","Return it through a safe school procedure and report where it was found.","Sell it and share the money.","Hide it until the owner stops asking."],1,"Integrity means acting honestly even when there is an opportunity to benefit unfairly."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Forgiveness and accountability","Two learners reconcile after one spreads a harmful rumour. What is a constructive sign that reconciliation is genuine?",["The harm is denied and no change occurs.","The learner acknowledges the harm, corrects the falsehood, apologises and changes future behaviour.","The injured learner is forced to stay silent.","Both learners pretend the issue never happened."],1,"Genuine reconciliation includes truth, responsibility, repair and changed conduct."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Faith and action","A learner regularly speaks about compassion but refuses to assist a classmate who has fallen and is injured. What ethical inconsistency does this show?",["Words and beliefs are not being matched by practical care.","Every person must be a medical expert.","Compassion is only a feeling and never requires action.","Helping others is always wrong."],0,"Christian ethics emphasises expressing compassion through appropriate practical action."],
["Christian Living","Prayer and reflection","A learner is anxious about a difficult decision. How can prayer and reflection be used responsibly?",["As a substitute for gathering facts and considering consequences.","Alongside reflection, seeking wise counsel, considering values and evaluating possible outcomes.","To guarantee that every preferred outcome will occur.","To avoid taking responsibility for any decision."],1,"Prayer and reflection can support discernment while the learner still considers evidence and responsibility."],
["Christian Living","Conflict resolution","Two groups disagree over how to share school resources. Which response best reflects peacemaking?",["Listen to each group, identify shared needs and agree on fair, transparent arrangements.","Allow the strongest group to take everything.","Spread rumours about both groups.","Avoid discussion until conflict becomes worse."],0,"Peacemaking includes listening, fairness, dialogue and practical steps to resolve disputes."],
["Christian Living","Service to others","A youth group wants to help families affected by flooding. Which plan best reflects thoughtful Christian service?",["Distribute whatever items are easiest to buy without asking what is needed.","Consult affected people, coordinate safe assistance, protect dignity and monitor whether support meets needs.","Publish private details of every family to attract attention.","Promise services the group cannot deliver."],1,"Effective service responds to actual needs, protects dignity and uses resources responsibly."],
["Christian Living","Responsible leadership","A student leader has to allocate limited funds for a class activity. What is the most ethical approach?",["Give the money to close friends without explanation.","Use clear criteria, consult the class, keep records and explain the decision.","Spend the money secretly.","Choose the most expensive option because it looks impressive."],1,"Responsible leadership involves fairness, transparency and accountability."],
["Christian Living","Self-control and decision-making","A learner is angry after an unfair comment and considers posting an insulting reply online. What is the wisest first step?",["Post immediately before thinking.","Pause, consider the consequences and choose a respectful response or seek help if needed.","Publish the other learner’s private information.","Ask friends to amplify the insult."],1,"Self-control helps prevent a temporary emotion from causing further harm."],
["Christian Living","Family responsibility","A family is under financial pressure and members disagree about spending. Which response best supports healthy relationships?",["Hide all spending and blame one member.","Discuss essential needs openly, make a realistic budget and share responsibilities fairly.","Borrow without discussing repayment.","Assume one person’s needs never matter."],1,"Honest communication and shared planning help families respond to pressure fairly."],
["Christian Living","Respectful disagreement","A learner disagrees with a classmate’s religious belief. Which response best reflects respect?",["Ridicule the belief publicly.","Listen respectfully, explain one’s own view without coercion and avoid discrimination.","Force the classmate to change belief.","Spread a false story about the classmate."],1,"Respectful dialogue allows disagreement without humiliation or coercion."],
["Christian Living","Work and responsibility","A learner is assigned a group task but does not complete it, leaving others to finish. Which value is most directly neglected?",["Responsibility and faithfulness to commitments.","The need to avoid all cooperation.","The importance of popularity.","The value of competition at any cost."],0,"Responsibility includes carrying out agreed duties and considering their effect on others."],
["Christian Living","Peace and reconciliation","A community dispute has continued for years. Which step may help begin reconciliation?",["Deny past harm and forbid discussion.","Create a safe process for listening, acknowledging harm, identifying shared interests and agreeing on practical steps.","Demand immediate agreement without hearing concerns.","Punish every person who asks a question."],1,"Reconciliation requires honest engagement, safety, recognition of harm and steps toward repair."],
["Church and Society","Care for vulnerable people","A church group learns that some elderly residents cannot reach a clinic. What response is most appropriate?",["Assume their families will solve every problem.","Consult the residents and relevant services, then organise safe transport or referrals within the group’s capacity.","Publish their medical records.","Offer unqualified medical treatment."],1,"Service should be needs-based, safe and coordinated with appropriate services."],
["Church and Society","Social justice","A local employer withholds wages from workers who have completed their work. Which Christian ethical principle is most relevant?",["Fairness and justice in treating workers and honouring obligations.","The idea that employers may ignore every agreement.","The belief that work has no value.","The rule that only wealthy people deserve payment."],0,"Justice includes fair treatment and honouring legitimate obligations to workers."],
["Church and Society","Peaceful civic engagement","A church youth group wants to address unsafe roads near a school. Which action best combines service and responsible citizenship?",["Collect evidence, consult residents and present a practical proposal to the responsible authority.","Damage road signs to attract attention.","Spread unverified allegations.","Ignore the people most affected."],0,"Constructive civic engagement uses evidence, consultation and lawful advocacy."],
["Church and Society","Stewardship of resources","A community project receives donations for school supplies. What practice best demonstrates stewardship?",["Keep no records to save time.","Record donations and spending, use funds for the agreed purpose and report outcomes to donors and beneficiaries.","Spend donations on personal needs.","Hide leftover supplies."],1,"Stewardship requires faithful use, transparency and accountability for resources entrusted to the group."],
["Church and Society","Inclusion","A community activity is planned in a building inaccessible to wheelchair users. What should organisers do?",["Proceed because inclusion is optional.","Review the venue and provide accessible arrangements so people can participate with dignity.","Ask wheelchair users to stay home.","Advertise the event as inclusive without making changes."],1,"Inclusion requires practical arrangements that allow participation, not only positive language."],
["Church and Society","Environmental action","A congregation notices plastic waste accumulating near a river. Which response is most responsible?",["Organise safe clean-up, reduce single-use materials and work with local authorities on prevention and disposal.","Burn all plastics in an open area.","Throw the waste into deeper water.","Blame one group without evidence."],0,"Service and stewardship include prevention, safe clean-up and cooperation on long-term solutions."],
["Christian Ethics","Truth and misinformation","A learner receives a sensational message accusing a community member of wrongdoing, but no evidence is provided. What is the ethical response?",["Forward it widely to warn everyone.","Verify the claim through reliable sources and avoid spreading unsubstantiated allegations.","Add more dramatic details.","Assume popularity proves truth."],1,"Truthfulness requires verification and care not to damage another person through unsupported claims."],
["Christian Ethics","Fairness and partiality","A club leader selects only friends for a funded trip despite other learners meeting the published criteria. Which concern is most direct?",["Partiality and unfair use of entrusted authority.","The value of teamwork.","The need for accurate weather forecasts.","The principle that all choices must be random."],0,"Fair leadership applies transparent criteria rather than favouritism."],
["Christian Ethics","Use of money","A learner borrows money from a classmate and then avoids all messages about repayment. Which ethical issue is involved?",["Honesty and responsibility in keeping commitments.","The belief that every debt disappears automatically.","The idea that friendship removes all obligations.","The need to avoid written communication."],0,"Borrowing creates a responsibility to communicate honestly and meet agreed terms."],
["Christian Ethics","Digital conduct","A learner shares a humiliating edited photograph of a classmate in a group chat. What is the most appropriate response from a bystander?",["Forward it to more groups.","Do not share it, support the targeted learner, report it through suitable channels and encourage removal.","Add a cruel caption.","Assume online actions cannot hurt people."],1,"Responsible digital conduct protects dignity and helps stop the spread of harm."],
["Christian Ethics","Corruption and honesty","A learner is offered a reward to change a competition result unfairly. What should the learner do?",["Accept it because the reward is useful.","Refuse, report the attempt through the proper process and preserve honest records.","Change the result but keep the reward secret.","Blame another learner without evidence."],1,"Integrity requires resisting dishonest influence and protecting fairness."],
["Christian Ethics","Forgiveness and boundaries","A person repeatedly bullies another and asks for forgiveness but continues the behaviour. What is the most balanced response?",["The harmed person must accept the behaviour without protection.","Forgiveness may be offered while seeking safety, accountability and evidence of changed behaviour.","Bullying is acceptable after an apology.","No one should ever ask for help."],1,"Forgiveness does not require tolerating ongoing harm; safety and accountability remain important."],
["Christian Ethics","Care for creation","A learner proposes wasting water because it is inexpensive. Which ethical response best reflects stewardship?",["Use resources without considering others.","Conserve water and consider community needs and environmental limits.","Assume natural resources are unlimited.","Pollute water because it can be replaced instantly."],1,"Stewardship involves responsible use of resources and care for other people and creation."],
["Christian Ethics","Human dignity and poverty","A charity wants to photograph recipients of food aid for publicity. What should it do?",["Photograph everyone without asking.","Obtain informed consent, protect privacy and present recipients with dignity rather than as objects of pity.","Publish names and addresses with the images.","Make aid conditional on agreeing to publicity."],1,"Ethical service respects dignity, consent and privacy."],
["Christian Ethics","Fair conflict resolution","Two learners accuse each other of damaging equipment. What is the fairest first step for a teacher or leader?",["Punish both immediately without checking facts.","Hear both accounts, review available evidence and follow a fair process before deciding.","Accept the first accusation automatically.","Post the dispute online."],1,"Fair process requires hearing relevant accounts and considering evidence before reaching a decision."],
["Christian Ethics","Honouring commitments","A group promises to deliver supplies by Friday but realises on Thursday that transport is unavailable. What is the responsible action?",["Say nothing and let beneficiaries wait.","Inform the affected people promptly, explain the situation honestly and agree on a realistic alternative.","Invent a delivery confirmation.","Blame beneficiaries for the delay."],1,"Honest communication and a realistic corrective plan honour responsibility even when plans change."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Compassion in practice","A learner sees a new student eating alone and being excluded from group work. Which action best expresses compassion?",["Assume the learner prefers isolation without checking.","Invite the learner to join, listen respectfully and help them participate without pressure.","Make the learner a public charity case.","Tell others not to speak to the learner."],1,"Compassion responds to possible exclusion with respectful, practical inclusion."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Humility","A group project succeeds, and one member claims all the credit despite others’ contributions. Which value is missing?",["Humility and recognition of others’ work.","The need to use accurate information.","The value of shared responsibility.","The need to communicate."],0,"Humility recognises contributions fairly rather than claiming others’ work."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Hope and resilience","After a community project fails to meet its target, the group reviews mistakes and develops a better plan. Which response is shown?",["Despair without reflection.","Resilience that combines hope with learning and responsible action.","A guarantee that every future project will succeed.","Avoidance of responsibility."],1,"Resilience includes learning from setbacks and taking constructive next steps."],
["Bible and Interpretation","Applying a teaching to context","A biblical teaching encourages care for strangers. A community receives displaced families after flooding. Which action best applies the principle?",["Exclude families because they are unfamiliar.","Coordinate safe, respectful support based on need and available resources.","Share private family details online.","Promise unlimited support without a plan."],1,"The principle is applied through practical hospitality that respects dignity and capacity."],
["Bible and Interpretation","Ethical interpretation","A learner quotes a single verse to justify an action that appears to contradict the broader message of the passage. What should be done?",["Accept the quotation without reading further.","Read the surrounding passage, consider its audience and compare the interpretation with the broader teaching.","Ignore the entire Bible.","Change the quotation to fit the argument."],1,"Contextual reading reduces the risk of using isolated words to support a misleading claim."],
["Church and Society","Community partnership","A church wants to support learners who struggle with reading. Which partnership is likely to be most effective?",["Work with teachers, families and learners to identify needs, provide suitable reading support and monitor progress.","Replace teachers without consulting the school.","Distribute unrelated gifts only.","Require learners to disclose private information publicly."],0,"Collaboration and monitoring help ensure that support responds to actual learning needs."],
["Christian Ethics","Responsible leadership","A leader discovers that a close friend has broken a safety rule during a community event. What should the leader do?",["Ignore it because of friendship.","Apply the safety process fairly, address the risk and avoid favouritism.","Hide the incident from organisers.","Punish an unrelated participant instead."],1,"Responsible leadership applies rules fairly and prioritises safety over personal loyalty."],
["Christian Living","Balancing obligations","A learner has a major assessment and has also promised to volunteer at an event. The schedules now conflict. What is the most responsible response?",["Disappear without telling anyone.","Communicate early, explain the conflict honestly and help arrange a suitable alternative if possible.","Blame the organisers after the event.","Ignore the assessment and the commitment without discussion."],1,"Responsible decision-making includes honest communication and reasonable efforts to honour commitments."],
["Christian Ethics","Respect for differences","A group includes people from different Christian traditions and some who are not Christian. Which practice best supports respectful cooperation on a community project?",["Require everyone to adopt one tradition before helping.","Agree on the shared service goal, respect conscience and treat participants fairly.","Exclude anyone who differs.","Mock participants’ beliefs."],1,"People can cooperate on a shared good while respecting differences in belief and conscience."],
["Christian Living","Personal responsibility","A learner makes a mistake that affects a group assignment. Which response best demonstrates responsibility?",["Hide the mistake and blame the group.","Acknowledge the mistake, help correct it and agree on steps to prevent recurrence.","Delete the group’s work.","Refuse to communicate."],1,"Taking responsibility includes acknowledging impact, repairing harm and learning from the mistake."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Justice for vulnerable groups","A community service plan gives assistance only to people who are easy to reach, although isolated households have greater need. What should the team reconsider?",["Whether the plan reaches people according to need and includes fair access for isolated households.","Whether isolated households should be ignored permanently.","Whether records should be destroyed.","Whether the easiest group should always receive everything."],0,"Justice requires assessing who is excluded and adapting service to reach those with greater need."]
["Christian Living","Discernment and wise counsel","A learner is unsure whether to join a group that pressures members to humiliate others. What is the wisest response?",["Join so as not to lose friends.","Reflect on the harm, seek trusted counsel and decline participation in harmful behaviour.","Keep quiet and participate secretly.","Assume group approval makes every action right."],1,"Discernment includes examining consequences and seeking wise counsel rather than following harmful pressure."],
["Christian Ethics","Use of authority","A youth leader controls access to donated supplies and gives them only to friends. Which ethical principle is most clearly violated?",["Fairness and stewardship of entrusted resources.","The need to organise meetings.","The value of keeping a calendar.","The idea that supplies need storage."],0,"A leader must use entrusted resources fairly for their intended purpose."],
["Church and Society","Voluntary service","A volunteer wants to help a family but the family declines a particular form of assistance. What should the volunteer do?",["Force the assistance on the family.","Listen to the family’s preferences and explore respectful alternatives without coercion.","Withdraw all help permanently as punishment.","Publish the family’s refusal online."],1,"Service should respect agency, dignity and consent."],
["Christian Faith and Values","Reconciliation and repair","A learner damages another person’s project and apologises. Which additional action best supports repairing the harm?",["Pretend the project was never damaged.","Offer practical help to repair or replace what was damaged and take steps to avoid repeating the mistake.","Demand immediate forgiveness.","Blame the person whose project was damaged."],1,"Repairing harm involves practical restitution and changed behaviour, not only words."]
];
